  "summary": "Times of San Diego analyzed La Mesa's Flock ALPR data showing 3.13 million searches over seven months on the city's 20 cameras, overwhelmingly driven by outside agencies in a 306-entity statewide network. The top querying agencies were CHP, Riverside County, SFPD, and LA/Orange County sheriffs, raising concerns about data sharing, oversight, and potential federal access, while La Mesa officials said they don't control outside agency query frequency.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "A single search conducted by an outside agency may simultaneously query numerous participating Flock networks. Consequently, these figures do not necessarily represent searches specifically directed at La Mesa's cameras or vehicles observed within the city.",
      "paragraph_idx": 11
    },
    {
      "quote": "once our data is shared, nothing can prevent the shared data from ending up in the hands of federal agencies, which endangers people and also violates California law. So, to prevent that, you'd have to just not share data with anyone.",
      "paragraph_idx": 22
    },
    {
      "quote": "More than 8% of the 3.13 million La Mesa searches from July 2025 through February were initiated by the California Highway Patrol.",
      "paragraph_idx": 19
    }
  ],
